WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
Hi ladies.

2
00:00:02.189 --> 00:00:03.260
Hello.
Hi.

3
00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.600
How are you today?

4
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.660
I'm doing pretty good.
This is so weird.

5
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.350
It's so weird.

6
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.970
This new world?

7
00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:11.803
Hi Sara.

8
00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:13.270
I know, it's like a whole new world,

9
00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.120
also it's kind of a dream world

10
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.030
'cause I feel like I'm always with Sara

11
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.370
and it's so nice

12
00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.698
to not have to be sitting next to you, Sara.

13
00:00:19.698 --> 00:00:22.330
(Drew laughs)

14
00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:26.510
I've been such big fans of yours for such a long time

15
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:30.550
and you guys have really had tremendous longevity

16
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:31.970
in your career.

17
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.330
And we also have in common that we all started

18
00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.690
at a young age and you know,

19
00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:43.080
what do you think that maybe you know now about life,

20
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.080
the industry, all of it, that maybe you didn't know then?

21
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.550
So many things (laughs).

22
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.590 line:15%
Well, one thing, this sounds like a bit of a cheesy thing

23
00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.340 line:15%
but someone told us

24
00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.330 line:15%
while we were probably 10 years into our career,

25
00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:04.330
they said a career is a marathon, it's not a sprint.

26
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.580
And I think in the beginning of our career,

27
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.280
we worried that like every decision we were making

28
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.580
could make or break us,

29
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.410
and we're 20 years into our career

30
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.360
and I still feel like making mistakes

31
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.420
is one of the most significant ways to grow and to change.

32
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.210
And I want to do this forever.

33
00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.810
And so, I wish I'd been a little easier on myself

34
00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.240
when I was younger.

35
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.110
It's so true.

36
00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.570
And I've been the kind of person

37
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.670
that thought every decision was life or death.

38
00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.560
I took everything so seriously, the tiniest little detail

39
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.840
of which I would obsess on was make or break.

40
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.970
I think for us the last four or five years

41
00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.500
our focus has been on making sure we tell the whole story

42
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.940 line:15%
you know, we aren't perfect

43
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.910 line:15%
and we haven't always been great at everything we do

44
00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.530 line:15%
and we weren't always awesome people

45
00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.850 line:15%
and we made lots of mistakes along the way

46
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.100
and had bad haircuts along the way.

47
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:56.933
And that's okay.

48
00:01:56.933 --> 00:01:59.299
Those are all the things that make up who we are.

49
00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:01.780
And yeah, I think that's been the biggest gift

50
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:02.640
of the last few years.

51
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.910
Maybe that's just age, too

52
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:07.480
but looking back has allowed us to sort of be less difficult

53
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.730
and hard on ourselves about that time.

54
00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.510
Was there a moment in your lives

55
00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:17.510
where you just thought you know what, life is good?

56
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.053
We have made it.

57
00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.800
We're in a moment where we're having

58
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.560
a lack of where we need to go.

59
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.960
And a moment of where we've been.

60
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.560
When I think about when it is

61
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.270
that I knew that we had made it

62
00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.650
there's a few things that pop into my head,

63
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.150
but one is my grandmother had this beautiful

64
00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:38.220
stainless steel fridge

65
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.768
and she never put anything on it, nothing,

66
00:02:39.768 --> 00:02:41.670
like there was never a thing,

67
00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.050
not one magnet, nothing,

68
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.620
but she kind of collected some of our clippings

69
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.640
and put it up on the fridge.

70
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.280
And I just remember, you know

71
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.080
really thinking that if our grandma was taking it seriously

72
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.350
that we should take it seriously.

73
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.140
But then I think about really big things

74
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:55.560
like playing on the Oscar stage.

75
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.310
I mean, we didn't think

76
00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.090
there was never a point in our adolescence

77
00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.970
or even in our twenties when I would have ever even

78
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.710
said out loud, oh, one day I want to play on the Oscars.

79
00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.400
That was just not something

80
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.230
that we would have imagined for ourselves.

81
00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.700
And I just feel so glad

82
00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.810
and really grateful that we had so much support

83
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.200
from our family because we've had a pretty cool run.

84
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.720
Well, speaking of family,

85
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.110
your mom is involved in your foundation.

86
00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:24.960
Will you quickly tell me about what she has brought to it?

87
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.810
So our mom is a therapist.

88
00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.080
She works with teenagers and couples

89
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.670
and she deals with relationships and she loves the work

90
00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.410
but she needs to blow off steam.

91
00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.400
So she started doing pottery

92
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.780
and she ended up being a natural

93
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:42.610
and she was inspired to do something with the pots

94
00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.180
that would benefit our foundation.

95
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.770
And so she made pots that have our hairstyles

96
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.910
from over the years.

97
00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.910
Yeah, we put up a, you know, this fundraiser for a week

98
00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.960
and Mom made 26 pots and we raised 15 grand.

99
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.610
And every year, my mom donates to the foundation,

100
00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:01.800
she's a monthly donator

101
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.910
but she kicked us off the year we started it.

102
00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.000
She donated $10,000

103
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.740
which let us send 10 LGBTQ kids to LGBTQ summer camp.

104
00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.730
And yeah, my mom's a big supporter and it's, you know,

105
00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.100
it's one of our most favorite things that we get to do,

106
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.690
which is give back to our community

107
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.210
and raise money for women and girls, so.

108
00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.210
Yeah, your foundation is so important

109
00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:22.820
and I just want to thank you

110
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.220
and High School, by the way, this is one of the first books

111
00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.870
I've ever seen in this format.

112
00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:32.520
And it's a true original as are you guys,

113
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.210
so it's ever so fitting that it is so unique

114
00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.270
and High School is available now in paperback.

115
00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:44.720
And thank you guys so much from the nineties to the aughts,

116
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.040
to the now 2021.

117
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.440
So wonderful to be living in your world.

118
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.648
Thank you, Tegan and Sara.

119
00:04:51.648 --> 00:04:54.231
(upbeat music)

